(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-cardio-pv-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which great vessel is the largest blood vessel in the human body, originating from the left ventricle and arching to the level of the sternal angle?","choices":["Aorta","Superior vena cava","Pulmonary artery","Inferior vena cava"],"answer":0,"exp":"The aorta is the body's largest vessel, originating from the LV (left ventricle) and arching at the sternal angle before descending posteriorly and to the left. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"The superior and inferior venae cavae drain venous blood from the body into which chamber?","choices":["Aorta","Left atrium","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both venae cavae empty venous blood into the right atrium along its medial border. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"The mediastinum, the central compartment of the thoracic cavity housing the heart and great vessels, is bounded anteriorly by which structure?","choices":["Clavicles","Diaphragm","Vertebral bodies","Sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mediastinum is bounded by the sternum anteriorly and the vertebral bodies posteriorly. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"Preload is best defined as:","choices":["The percentage of ventricular volume ejected during each heartbeat","The intrinsic ability of cardiac muscle to contract forcefully at a given preload","The initial stretching of cardiac muscle fibers prior to contraction","The resistance the ventricles face when ejecting blood during systole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Preload is the initial stretch of the myocardial fibers before contraction, influenced by venous return. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which factor is most directly influenced by vascular tone in the aorta and peripheral arteries, along with the blood volume/pressure already present in the aorta?","choices":["Preload","Afterload","Myocardial contractility","Ejection fraction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Afterload is the resistance the ventricle must overcome to eject blood, dictated by aortic/peripheral vascular tone and pre-existing aortic pressure. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Myocardial contractility is most enhanced by which of the following?","choices":["Prolonged AV (atrioventricular) nodal conduction delay slowing ventricular filling","Stimulation by the sympathetic nervous system and adequate myocardial oxygen delivery","Increased afterload from significant peripheral arterial vasoconstriction","Increased venous return occurring specifically during deep inspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation and adequate blood flow/oxygen delivery to the myocardium enhance contractility. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which valve's closure sound is best heard at the left fourth intercostal space at or near the lower left sternal border?","choices":["Tricuspid valve (S1)","Mitral valve (S1)","Aortic valve (S2)","Pulmonic valve (S2)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tricuspid area for S1 is located at the left fourth intercostal space near the lower sternal border. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Systole is best described as:","choices":["The period when both AV (atrioventricular) and semilunar valves are simultaneously open","The period of ventricular contraction, pumping blood from the ventricles into the arteries","The interval between the P wave and QRS complex on ECG (electrocardiogram)","The phase when the heart muscle fully relaxes and the atria passively fill with blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systole is ventricular contraction, ejecting blood from the ventricles into the arterial system. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"On the right side of the heart during systole, which valve opens to allow blood into the pulmonary artery, and which closes to prevent regurgitation?","choices":["Pulmonic valve opens; tricuspid valve closes","Pulmonic valve closes; tricuspid valve opens","Both valves open simultaneously","Both valves remain closed"],"answer":0,"exp":"During systole, the pulmonic valve opens to eject blood into the pulmonary artery while the tricuspid valve closes to prevent regurgitation into the right atrium. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why do peripheral arm and leg pulses lag behind actual ventricular contraction?","choices":["Because the pressure wave travels faster than actual blood flow, but still takes time to reach the periphery, delaying the palpable pulse relative to contraction","Because peripheral arteries lack any meaningful elastic recoil, which would otherwise be needed to propagate the pressure wave forward toward the periphery without delay","Because delayed venous return to the heart secondarily slows the arterial pulse wave as it propagates outward toward the distal periphery","There is no lag whatsoever; peripheral pulses are precisely and perfectly synchronous with ventricular contraction at every single heartbeat"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pressure wave moves rapidly through the arterial tree but still lags contraction, making peripheral pulses less reliable for precise cardiac cycle timing. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"On the Littmann Cardiology IV, which side of the stethoscope is used for pediatric patients, checking for carotid bruits, and checking around bandages?","choices":["Neither side; a separate pediatric stethoscope is required","The small side","The large side","Either side interchangeably"],"answer":1,"exp":"The small side is used for pediatric patients, carotid bruit auscultation, and listening around bandages. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the normal range for pulse pressure?","choices":["30–40 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","10–20 mm Hg","50–70 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal pulse pressure is generally 30–40 mm Hg. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"A family history of which finding is specifically concerning for cardiac history?","choices":["Osteoarthritis of the knees","Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Recurrent migraine headaches","Heart problems or sudden death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Family history of heart problems or sudden death is specifically listed as concerning cardiac family history. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which of the following past medical history elements raises concern for cardiac disease?","choices":["History of childhood tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy surgery","Irregular heartbeat, high cholesterol, vascular disease, or diabetes","History of longstanding corrective lens use for nearsightedness","History of routine seasonal influenza vaccination administered annually"],"answer":1,"exp":"These are specifically listed as concerning past medical history elements for cardiac disease. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"During palpation of the carotid artery, which technique is correct?","choices":["Press directly over both carotid bifurcations simultaneously using both index fingers at once, comparing pulse amplitude on each side","Palpate the carotid pulse only after the patient stands fully upright and performs a sustained Valsalva maneuver throughout the exam","Press just inside the medial border of the relaxed SCM (sternocleidomastoid) at the level of the cricoid cartilage, using the contralateral hand's fingers","Use the ipsilateral hand's thumb pressed firmly over the mastoid process while the patient turns their head sharply away from the examiner"],"answer":2,"exp":"The examiner uses the right fingers for the left carotid and left fingers for the right carotid, pressing just inside the medial SCM (sternocleidomastoid) border at the cricoid cartilage level. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which finding might be detected during carotid palpation, according to the lecture?","choices":["Pulsus tardus only, never other findings","S3 and S4 gallops","Pulsus alternans or pulsus paradoxus","Splitting of S2"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists pulsus alternans, pulsus paradoxus, vibrations, and thrills as possible findings on carotid palpation. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"During palpation of the precordium for heaves and thrills, which location corresponds to Erb's point?","choices":["4th intercostal space, left sternal border","3rd intercostal space, left sternal border","2nd intercostal space, right sternal border","5th intercostal space, left midclavicular line"],"answer":1,"exp":"Erb's point is located at the 3rd intercostal space along the left sternal border. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"When examining a patient with breasts and searching for the apical impulse, what should you ask the patient to do?","choices":["Lie completely flat with arms at the sides","Sit upright and lean forward","Hold their breath in full inspiration","Displace the breast upward or laterally"],"answer":3,"exp":"Asking the patient to displace the breast tissue upward or laterally helps expose the apical impulse location for palpation. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"Approximately what fraction of circulating blood volume can the venous system hold?","choices":["Up to 1/10","Nearly all circulating blood volume","Up to 1/3","Up to 2/3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Veins are capable of holding up to two-thirds of the circulating blood volume. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"The thoracic duct empties lymph fluid from most of the body into the junction of which vessels?","choices":["The superior and inferior venae cavae directly","The right internal jugular and right subclavian veins","The left internal jugular and left subclavian veins","The pulmonary veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thoracic duct drains into the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"The right lymphatic duct drains fluid from which regions?","choices":["The entire body below the level of the diaphragm bilaterally","Only the right lower extremity below the inguinal ligament","The left side of the head, neck, and entire upper thorax","The right side of the head, neck, thorax, and right upper limb"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right lymphatic duct specifically drains the right side of the head, neck, thorax, and right upper limb. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"When inspecting the upper and lower extremities, what is the recommended extent of inspection?","choices":["Only the single extremity that has reported symptoms or complaints currently","Only the proximal joints located nearest the trunk of the body, not distally","Both arms from fingertips to shoulders, and both legs from groin/buttock down to the feet","Only the distal hands and feet, excluding the remainder of each limb entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Full inspection spans fingertips to shoulders for the arms and groin/buttock to feet for the legs. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which symptom, if reported provoked by exertion, is relevant to peripheral vascular history?","choices":["Pain or cramping in the extremities (claudication-type symptoms)","Joint locking or catching provoked by twisting motion","Headache provoked specifically by bright, flickering light","Sore throat provoked specifically by breathing cold air"],"answer":0,"exp":"Symptoms provoked by exertion, such as extremity pain/cramping, are specifically relevant to PV (peripheral vascular) history and suggest possible claudication. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which of the following is part of the peripheral vascular exam of the upper extremities?","choices":["Auscultating for bowel sounds in all four abdominal quadrants","Assessing visual fields by confrontation testing bilaterally","Testing deep tendon reflexes at the biceps and triceps tendons","Palpating the epitrochlear nodes and the superficial inguinal lymph nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Both epitrochlear and superficial inguinal nodes are listed among the peripheral vascular exam components. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where is the posterior tibial pulse palpated?","choices":["Midway between the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) and symphysis pubis","In the popliteal fossa behind the knee","Lateral to the extensor tendon of the great toe on the dorsum of the foot","Behind and slightly below the medial malleolus of the ankle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior tibial pulse is felt by curving the fingers behind and slightly below the medial malleolus. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where is the dorsalis pedis pulse palpated, and what anatomic variant should the examiner be aware of?","choices":["In the popliteal fossa located behind the knee; it is essentially never congenitally absent in any patient","On the dorsum of the foot just lateral to the extensor tendon of the great toe; it may be congenitally absent or branch higher","On the plantar surface of the heel, positioned just medial to the prominent calcaneal tuberosity landmark itself","Directly behind the medial malleolus of the ankle joint; it is always reliably present in absolutely every single patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct location, and the artery may be congenitally absent or branch higher in the ankle in some patients. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"If the dorsalis pedis pulse cannot be felt at its typical location, what should the examiner do?","choices":["Immediately conclude the patient has entirely absent arterial flow to that particular foot altogether","Explore the dorsum of the foot more laterally, since the artery may branch higher or take an anomalous course","Move on to auscultating the abdomen for bowel sounds and vascular bruits in all quadrants instead","Apply firm, sustained pressure directly over the medial malleolus of the ankle joint instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because the dorsalis pedis can be congenitally absent or branch higher, exploring more laterally on the dorsum may locate it. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"When palpating pulses in the upper extremities bilaterally, what should the examiner specifically compare?","choices":["Only the presence of a bruit in each separate arm","Skin temperature only, never the pulse quality itself","The pulses in both arms, assessing amplitude","Capillary refill time only, in each fingertip"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture specifically instructs comparing the pulses in both arms and assessing amplitude. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why do clinicians assess peripheral pulses, according to the lecture?","choices":["To check heart rate and rhythm and to assess blood flow","Only for documentation purposes with no clinical value","Only to roughly estimate the patient's systemic blood pressure","Only to determine the patient's overall hydration status"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture lists checking heart rate/rhythm and assessing blood flow as the two main reasons providers assess pulses. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"A capillary refill time greater than 2–3 seconds (delayed) may suggest which of the following?","choices":["Reduced peripheral perfusion, though confounding factors must also be considered","A completely normal finding requiring absolutely no further clinical consideration","Definitive proof of arterial occlusion with no other possible explanation","An expected, unremarkable finding occurring only in pediatric patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Delayed refill may suggest reduced peripheral perfusion, but the lecture notes temperature, age, lighting, PVD (peripheral vascular disease), and technique can also affect the result. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 52"}]};
